WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
 So now I want to talk about the concept of logic gates.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.320
 So what are logic gates?

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.760
 Well logic gates are not a real thing.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.240
 Transistors are a real thing.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.920
 A logic gate is an abstraction that we use

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.880
 as hardware designers to greatly simplify our lives.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.760
 This is no different than when I was teaching you software

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.800
 and I'm like, look at this cool low level way

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:23.160
 of doing this, that, and the other thing.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.880
 And you're like, oh, that's really pretty complex.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.480
 And then in the next breath, I say,

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.040
 well, if we extend this object,

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.320
 we get all this work for free.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.440
 And now our code goes from 75 lines down to two lines.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.480
 And that's kind of how abstractions work.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.080
 And that's how gates work.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.680
 So gates, we don't need to think about sort

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.480
 of response graphs of input voltages and output voltages.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:45.320
 We don't have to think about that.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.160
 That still is a thing.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.600
 Gates don't make the whole analog problem go away.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.600
 They just make it so we don't think about it anymore.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.280
 And that's a problem that the folks

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.360
 who manufacture these chips have solved

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:00.840
 in the manufacturing process.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.040
 We pretty much say, let's make a gate

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:05.520
 and then somehow the gates are manufactured

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.520
 out of analog components.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:10.240
 The way we think about gates is beautiful.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.160
 We think about zeros and ones,

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.080
 which is very much digital logic.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.480
 And the concept of digital logic predates computers.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:17.600
 Absolutely.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.600
 And so we just say, oh, this gate, this not gate,

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.720
 if presented with an input of zero, gives an output of one.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.480
 And if it's presented with an input of one,

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.360
 it gives an output of zero.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:29.480
 And that's a not gate.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.440
 And I like starting with a not gate.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:32.760
 First is the simplest thing.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.000
 We can see how it's built from transistors,

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.560
 but it also is like, it's active.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:38.640
 It's doing something.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.680
 It's not just copying something.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.600
 So we'll build much more complex logic circuits.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.120
 We're going to see much more complex gates.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:50.840
 But I can't really run away from history quite yet.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:56.480
 Over time, as manufacturing capabilities changed

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:59.120
 from PMOS to NMOS to CMOS,

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:02.240
 the way we built a logic gate underneath the abstraction,

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:04.600
 all these logic gates have an input of A

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.320
 and an output of out.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.520
 If you put a zero at A, you get a one at out.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.400
 If you could put a one at A, you get a zero at out.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.240
 And so there are three symbols, like the VDD,

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:17.880
 there's like a nine volt battery

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.280
 that has its positive terminal at VDD,

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.520
 and it's negative terminal at the little ground symbol

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.120
 at the bottom.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.480
 The other thing we see is a PMOS transistor.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.920
 At the top there, it's a transistor.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.360
 It's kind of implying that there's a switch going on

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.440
 or a valve if you want.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.200
 The little circle says PMOS

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.400
 and the lack of little circle says NMOS.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.400
 And then the bottom thing is a resistor.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.600
 And it's probably easiest to talk about these

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.040
 in the concept of how they work.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:46.920
 So if you present a zero,

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.600
 let's start with the PMOS in 1972.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:53.040
 You present a zero at the input.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:58.040
 That means that the PMOS transistor conducts.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:01.680
 And so that means that the positive voltage

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.840
 is copied to output.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.560
 So when you press a zero, it conducts and it goes to output.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.160
 The PMOS transistor is now conducting

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.800
 both to get the voltage to the output

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.600
 to produce a one at the output,

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:16.760
 but also connecting through the resistor to ground.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:18.720
 Now, every time you see a resistor,

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.000
 it's kind of like a pack.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.880
 And the problem is, is resistors heat up.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.200
 But if you didn't have that resistor there,

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.840
 all the little lines, the wires, the tiny wires,

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.680
 and perhaps even the transistor, they would melt.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.360
 So the resistors are there to protect the transistors, okay?

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:37.080
 And protect the wires, frankly.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:40.240
 When NMOS is presented with a zero,

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.600
 that means it's an open circuit.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.000
 And so the nice thing there is,

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.560
 when you present it with a zero,

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.080
 it just copies through a resistor,

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:50.400
 the voltage to the output.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:51.400
 And the cool thing there is,

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.440
 it's not really sending as much current to the output,

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:55.280
 because all that output is being as connected

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:57.840
 to the input of another transistor later in the circuit.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.880
 And so that doesn't really heat up so badly.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.240
 Now, if you look at CMOS,

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:06.840
 what you have is a PMOS transistor at the top

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.280
 and an NMOS transistor at the bottom.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.440
 And they work in pairs.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.840
 And so in the case of the zero,

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:17.160
 the PMOS transistor conducts and the NMOS transistor

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:18.000
 doesn't conduct.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.240
 And so there is no resistor

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:23.480
 and the voltage is copied to the output.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:27.600
 Now, let's look at the case of the one.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.160
 In PMOS, when you present a one,

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.000
 the PMOS is an open circuit.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.720
 And so that means that we need the output to be zero,

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:40.120
 which means we have to connect the ground to the output.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:42.680
 That is connected through a resistor,

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:44.760
 but it's low current because the output

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.160
 is just going to the input of another transistor.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.280
 And so if you look then at the NMOS,

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:51.560
 we have the opposite problem

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:52.920
 when we were presented with a one

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:55.520
 because the NMOS then conducts,

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:59.120
 it both conducts the ground to the output

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:02.960
 and it conducts the positive to the ground.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:04.240
 But that would be a short circuit

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:05.880
 unless we put a resistor there.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:08.560
 So there you got a thing heating up.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:11.360
 So the problem is with PMOS and NMOS,

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:14.040
 one of the two states for both of them

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:16.360
 is going to be causing heat.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:18.760
 One is causing heat on a zero,

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:20.280
 the other is causing heat on a one.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.560
 But then we look at the CMOS, okay?

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.600
 And what we see when we present a one

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.160
 and we want an output of zero,

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:30.800
 well, the NMOS transistor at the bottom is conducting

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:32.720
 and so ground is connected to the output,

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:34.480
 which is exactly what we want.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.200
 And the PMOS transistor is an open circuit.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.440
 So the voltage VDD is not being connected.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:43.240
 And we don't need a resistor.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:45.880
 And so what you see is there are no resistors

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:48.760
 and there is just, you have to be able to build

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:52.520
 at a low level, both PMOS and NMOS transistors

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:56.120
 in the same circuit, like really tiny.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:01.120
 And so what happened was is NMOS and PMOS were hot

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:05.480
 and the hotter they were, the less dense the chips could be.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:07.120
 So once we went to CMOS, and again,

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:10.320
 that was really not a question of wanting it,

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:14.120
 it's a question of practical manufacturing, okay?

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:19.120
 And so at some point, mid 1980s, Intel 486,

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:22.400
 CMOS became practical, all of a sudden,

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:24.400
 you didn't have to have a central processor

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:26.320
 the size of a large cabinet.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:27.920
 And part of that large cabinet had to do

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:29.080
 with how much power it was taking

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:30.800
 and how much heat it was generating.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:31.960
 If you put it all together,

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:33.440
 it would just like instantly melt.

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.640
 And so you had to kind of take these transistors

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:39.480
 and keep them and resistors in NMOS and PMOS computers

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:41.000
 and keep them away from each other

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:43.800
 because they would overheat because of the resistors.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:50.280
 But the moment that you can take the size of a computer

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:55.160
 from 10 feet end to end to one inch end to end,

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:57.920
 everything got faster.

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:01.320
 So in 1983 is a long time ago,

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:04.920
 there hasn't been a major like upheaval in,

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:07.280
 they talk about like quantum computing and stuff,

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.640
 but, and that would be a major upheaval at this level,

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:13.000
 but really from 1983 to present,

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:14.960
 we just pretty much make CMOS

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:18.720
 because once we could manufacture it reliably,

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:20.720
 we'll do CMOS, okay?

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:25.200
 So now what I want to show you is a tool that I built

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.280
 called a CMOS layout tool.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:29.840
 And so this way you can connect them

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.560
 and put them together and simulate circuits.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:35.080
 So let's take a look at this tool.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:37.960
 Okay, across the top,

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:40.600
 we have a number of things that you can place in this circuit

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.800
 and then we'll draw wires in between them.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:45.280
 So let's start with a probe.

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:46.640
 Here's a probe.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:50.280
 And if we connect the probe to the positive,

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:53.480
 you see that the probe is reading

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:55.400
 that now it's got five volts.

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:59.400
 Now, if we delete that and we place a probe

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:02.880
 and connect it to the negative, it's zero.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:05.120
 So part of what we're going to do in the NOT gate

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:08.920
 is have a switch as input.

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:12.000
 A switch is in effect a switch

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.600
 that either goes to the positive or the ground.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:18.440
 And you can imagine there's a wire running up here

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:19.680
 and a wire running down there,

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:21.040
 but I didn't show that.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:23.320
 It's just sort of like another little,

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:25.720
 it's connected either to the top or the bottom

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:27.600
 so that we can test our circuits.

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:29.640
 So what we're going to do is build a circuit

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:35.520
 that can create the logic so that whatever we have here

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:38.480
 as input, we're going to have output

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:40.440
 that is the opposite of that.

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:44.000
 So these, after a while you get used to these.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:47.640
 So let's first put a PMOS transistor at the top

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:50.960
 and then put a NMOS transistor below it.

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:54.840
 So we're going to connect the voltage

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:00.760
 to the top connector of the PMOS transistor,

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:05.120
 and then we're going to connect the bottom connection

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:07.680
 of the NMOS transistor to ground,

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.840
 and then we're going to take the output

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:11.400
 of the NMOS transistor

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:13.760
 and connect it to the PMOS transistor.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:16.080
 Now I say PMOS, the little circle is PMOS,

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:18.920
 and the lack of the little circle is NMOS.

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:22.440
 And so the output is going to be connected,

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:24.540
 it can be connected either place,

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:28.200
 but the output is basically connected

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:31.680
 to the connection between the two transistors.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:33.760
 And then the last thing we have to do

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:37.040
 is connect the switch to the grid

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:41.760
 of each of these transistors, and now we have it.

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:45.600
 So you can see that it's been sort of adapting and changing.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:49.880
 And the key thing is that the PMOS and NMOS transistors

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:51.840
 have kind of opposite logic.

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:56.040
 So the PMOS here is presented with a five volts,

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:57.440
 which means it's an open circuit,

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:00.600
 and the NMOS is presented with five volts,

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:01.760
 which means it's a closed circuit.

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:03.840
 And that's what this little green line is saying,

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.480
 that it's a closed circuit.

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:10.480
 So if I flip this, it means that the PMOS transistor conducts

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:14.720
 and the NMOS transistor no longer conducts.

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:17.640
 Okay, and so now the output is five volts

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:19.700
 because the five volts is flowing

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:22.720
 through the PMOS transistor to the output.

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:26.440
 If I flip it, the PMOS transistor opens up

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:29.600
 and the ground flows through the NMOS transistor

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:32.680
 to the output.

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:35.780
 And so that's it, and that's the picture

00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:38.840
 that I've been showing you that shows the PMOS and NMOS,

00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:40.200
 and there are no resistors in here,

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:42.000
 so there's no dangerous heat.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:45.540
 So I can flip this as fast as I like.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:47.360
 And you can see it flipping back and forth,

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:51.560
 and it's really activating or closing a closed circuit,

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:54.080
 a circuit to close on either the NMOS or the PMOS,

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:55.480
 but we've constructed it in a way

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:59.920
 that it's like an if-then-else for circuit transmission.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:02.400
 And that's why I kind of call this NOT gate

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:04.640
 an electrical if statement.

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:06.560
 So basically that was a NOT gate,

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:09.520
 and we're going to build much more complex digital gates,

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:11.480
 but then we're going to build circuits out of those gates.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:15.040
 We're not really going to build very complex circuits

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:16.120
 out of transistors,

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:19.240
 although ultimately they're manufactured as transistors.

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:21.820
 And so there is mathematical logic

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:24.920
 that predates computers for AND and OR,

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:26.960
 exclusive OR and NOT.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:31.960
 And then there's other logic that we kind of learn to use

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:34.120
 because we could build them with fewer transistors,

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:37.960
 NAND and NOR, and NAND is NOT AND, and NOR is NOR.

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:42.280
 So our classic logic gates come from digital logic,

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:44.120
 something that was a theoretical thing

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:46.720
 that there was a pen and paper

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:49.160
 well before computers came on the scene.

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:52.600
 So the first digital logic gate is a NOT gate

00:11:52.600 --> 00:11:54.120
 that simply inverts its input.

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:56.480
 Zero becomes one, one becomes zero.

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:58.560
 An AND gate is simple.

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:01.120
 If both of its inputs are one,

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:04.400
 then the output is one, otherwise the output is zero.

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:07.340
 The OR gate is if either of its inputs is one,

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:08.640
 the output is one.

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:12.040
 And if both inputs are zero, the output is zero.

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:13.560
 And then there's the exclusive OR gate,

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:14.880
 which we're going to find really useful.

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:18.520
 It's kind of a, it almost like counts its inputs.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:21.240
 It's not a counter in any way, but it counts its inputs.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:24.280
 If one of the inputs is one, its output is one.

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:27.080
 If both the inputs are zero or both the inputs are one,

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:30.720
 the output of an exclusive OR gate is zero.

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:35.720
 Now, it turns out that we tended to use those AND, OR,

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:41.200
 and NOT in classic digital logic math,

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:45.200
 but it turns out that it's easier to build NAND and NOR,

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:47.740
 which is really an inversion of the AND

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:49.160
 and an inversion of the NOR.

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:51.720
 Now, you could throw a NOT gate on the end of these things

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:53.400
 to make them be ANDs and ORs,

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:56.020
 but it turned out that early times,

00:12:56.020 --> 00:12:58.680
 we just could build NANDs and NORs

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:00.760
 with a lot fewer transistors.

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:04.120
 And so the NAND gate is the opposite of AND,

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:08.320
 which means that if any of the inputs of a NAND is zero,

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:09.440
 the output is a one.

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:12.000
 And if both the inputs of a NAND are one,

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:13.000
 the output is a zero.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:14.420
 And similarly for the OR.

00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:16.960
 If both the inputs are zero, the output's a one.

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:20.560
 And if any of the inputs of the NOR are one,

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:21.660
 the output is a zero.

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:26.280
 It is literally NOT AND and NOT OR,

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:28.920
 and can be turned into AND and OR with one more NOT gate,

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:31.800
 which means that you would put two more transistors

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:34.740
 at the end of each of these things.

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:38.880
 I want to show you how we can lay out a NOR gate.

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:41.160
 And the basic idea of these things

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:43.280
 is that it's kinda like a NOT gate,

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:44.920
 and that's what's cool about it.

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:48.720
 Except there are two PMOS transistors at the top

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:51.200
 and two NMOS transistors at the bottom.

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:54.900
 The bottom set of transistors decide

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:56.360
 when it's connected to ground,

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:58.080
 and the top set of transistors decide

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:00.640
 when it's connected to five volts.

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:04.120
 And they work in kinda parallel and opposite

00:14:04.120 --> 00:14:06.460
 to make sure that there's never a connection

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:10.200
 from the voltage to the ground.

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:11.960
 It's either the ground to the output

00:14:11.960 --> 00:14:13.720
 or the voltage to the output.

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:17.860
 So let's do a real quick walkthrough of a NOR gate.

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:19.800
 So NOT OR.

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:22.680
 So right now we have inputs of one.

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:26.480
 Now we've made the input zero, so when it's zero,

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:28.580
 both are zeros, then the output is a one.

00:14:28.580 --> 00:14:32.600
 And if any of them are one, either of them are one,

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:34.520
 then the output becomes zero.

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:36.920
 And so it's not that tricky.

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:40.360
 It sorta looks like an extended NOT gate

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:45.360
 in that basically we've got two PMOS transistors at the top,

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:48.500
 and if either of them are one,

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:51.840
 that blocks the flow of electricity

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:54.920
 from the five volts to the output.

00:14:54.920 --> 00:15:00.800
 And the same is true, we have in parallel NMOS gates

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:02.740
 where if either of them is on,

00:15:02.740 --> 00:15:05.520
 that connects ground to the output.

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:08.720
 So you can see that when I have them both,

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:12.280
 with both of them on, both A and B on,

00:15:12.280 --> 00:15:17.280
 you see that both of the NMOS transistors are conducting

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:20.200
 and so ground comes up.

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:22.600
 But like if I just change one of them,

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:26.300
 you see that the five volts is flowing through

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:30.560
 the top PMOS, but it's being stopped by the second PMOS.

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:33.680
 And so that means that the plus five

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:35.320
 does not flow to the output.

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:38.840
 And looking at the NMOS transistors,

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:42.120
 you see that this one is not conducting,

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:45.440
 but the other one, the B input is conducting.

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:50.440
 And so there's at least one path from the output to ground.

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:55.560
 If I flip these, you'll see that now that A is one

00:15:56.040 --> 00:16:00.800
 and B is zero, you see that the plus five is blocked

00:16:00.800 --> 00:16:05.000
 by the non-conducting first PMOS transistor

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:09.440
 and the ground passes through the conducting NMOS transistor,

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:12.380
 the first NMOS, the A NMOS transistor.

00:16:12.380 --> 00:16:15.640
 And so just kind of look at it,

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:18.440
 you see that what it is is really just

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:21.040
 kind of more not gates that has the,

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:23.240
 it's either going to conduct eventually from the top

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:27.000
 or the bottom, and now we can have all the combinations

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:31.120
 of the inputs that work and by creating this structure.

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:35.080
 And so you could create a three input NOR gate,

00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:38.880
 a four input NOR gate, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:41.560
 So this is just a look at an upcoming lecture

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:43.560
 and an upcoming tool that you're going to use.

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:47.020
 Now, once we have the abstraction of digital logic,

00:16:47.020 --> 00:16:51.160
 we can start designing circuits using digital logic design.

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:54.080
 And all of a sudden, it just is a lot simpler

00:16:54.080 --> 00:16:55.120
 and a lot less cluttered.

00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:59.300
 So here is a NAND gate designed at the level of a gate

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:01.440
 with inputs and outputs.

00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:03.160
 And you're going to like it.

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:05.360
 Well, we're not going to build complex circuits

00:17:05.360 --> 00:17:06.800
 using transistors.

00:17:06.800 --> 00:17:10.720
 All we're trying to do right now is give you the mapping

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:14.200
 from transistors to gates so that later when we use gates,

00:17:14.200 --> 00:17:15.760
 you just assume that it's okay

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:17.840
 that there are transistors underneath.

00:17:17.840 --> 00:17:20.420
